WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 230: The Netherlands: William the Silent, the Dutch Revolt, and the Sea Beggars

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.000
[Rebecca] Welcome back to most fascinating stories.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:16.080
[Rebecca] Today, we journey to the low-lying windswept marshes and North Sea coastlines of the Low Countries to follow one of history's most inspiring battles for human freedom.

3
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:23.440
[Rebecca] The Dutch Revolt, William the Silent, and the Birth of the Dutch Republic.

4
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:36.240
[George] In the mid-16th century, the seventeen provinces of the Netherlands were the wealthiest commercial crossroads in Europe, a bustling tapestry of vibrant merchant cities like Antwerp, Amsterdam, and Ghent.

5
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:42.720
[George] But in 1556, these fiercely independent provinces fell under the direct rule of King Philip I.

6
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:52.560
[George] of Spain, a devout, rigid Catholic monarch determined to eradicate Protestant heresy and impose absolute Spanish central authority upon the lowlands.

7
00:00:52.560 --> 00:01:05.200
[George] When Calvinist Protestants staged public iconoclastic riots in 1566, smashing statues in churches across the provinces, Philip reacted with terrifying, merciless vengeance.

8
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:16.720
[George] He dispatched his most ruthless general, Fernando Alvarez de Toledo, the iron-willed Duke of Alba, at the head of 10,000 hardened Spanish veterans to occupy the Netherlands.

9
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:35.680
[George] Alba established the Council of Troubles, soon christened by terrified locals as the Council of Blood, an inquisitorial military tribunal that arrested thousands of citizens, condemned 18,000 heretics and rebels to death by beheading or fire, and confiscated millions of guilders in private property.

10
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:42.400
[George] A dark pall of terror fell over the Low Countries, driving thousands of merchants and artisans into exile.

11
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:55.360
[George] The public squares of Brussels and Antwerp became scenes of continuous public executions, where beloved nobles like the Counts of Egmont and Horn were beheaded on black scaffolds for daring to advocate for local liberties.

12
00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:05.320
[George] The Duke of Alba's iron fisted rule alienated Catholic and Protestant burghers alike, who saw their municipal charters trampled by foreign Spanish garrisons.

13
00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:16.680
[George] The Spanish army of Flanders was the most formidable military machine of its age, but its excessive cruelty and heavy taxation unified the disparate Dutch provinces in common resistance.

14
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:27.080
[George] The one nobleman possessing the courage and stature to resist Spanish tyranny was William of Nassau, Prince of Orange, affectionately remembered as William the Silent.

15
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:41.080
[George] Born into German nobility and raised in the Catholic court of Emperor Charles V in Brussels, William earned his nickname not because he never spoke, but because he possessed extraordinary diplomatic discretion and emotional self-control.

16
00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:53.240
[George] As royal stadholder of Holland and Zealand, William observed the horrors of the Council of Blood with growing revulsion, declaring that he could not approve of princes wishing to rule over the consciences of their people.

17
00:02:53.240 --> 00:03:04.920
[George] When the Duke of Alba summoned him to Brussels, William wisely fled to Germany, selling his ancestral estates, silver and jewels to raise a mercenary army to liberate the provinces.

18
00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:10.360
[George] His early military campaigns were disastrous failures against Alba's veteran Tercios.

19
00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:20.120
[George] Yet William never surrendered, adopting as his personal motto It is not necessary to hope in order to undertake, nor to succeed in order to persevere.

20
00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:26.760
[George] He converted to Calvinism and committed his entire fortune, honor, and life to the cause of Dutch freedom.

21
00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:39.480
[George] From his exile in Dillenburg Castle, William directed an extensive international diplomatic and financial network, rallying Protestant princes in Germany and England to support the struggling Dutch cause.

22
00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:44.880
[George] William of Orange's famous Apology of 1580 was a blistering indictment of Philip I.

23
00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:50.000
[George] I's tyranny, circulated in thousands of copies across the printing presses of Europe.

24
00:03:50.240 --> 00:04:02.320
[George] William the Silent spent his personal fortune purchasing arms, gunpowder, and grain for his troops, mortgaging his ancestral castles and accepting financial ruin for the liberation of the Netherlands.

25
00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:06.480
[George] The spark that ignited the successful revolution came from the sea.

26
00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:19.120
[George] Desperate Dutch privateers, exiled from their ports and branded by Spanish authorities as Les Gieux or the Beggars, formed an audacious maritime guerrilla force known as the Waterjuzen, or sea beggars.

27
00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:30.480
[George] Wearing silver crescent badges engraved with the defiant motto, rather Turkish than Popish, these rough sea dogs raided Spanish shipping and coastal outposts across the North Sea.

28
00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:46.160
[George] On April 1, 1572, a fleet of twenty four sea beggar ships commanded by the fierce nobleman William de la Marque, driven by adverse winds into the mouth of the Meuse River, made a surprise assault on the lightly defended port town of Brielle.

29
00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:52.400
[George] The beggars captured the town, threw open the gates, and proclaimed the authority of the Prince of Orange.

30
00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.640
[George] The capture of Briel sent an electrifying shockwave across the Netherlands.

31
00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:12.640
[George] Within weeks, cities across Holland and Zeeland, including Flushing, Dordrecht, and Harlem, rose in open revolt against the Spanish garrisons, throwing their gates open to the Prince of Orange and giving the rebellion its first permanent territorial base.

32
00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:25.440
[George] The popular rhyme on April Fool's Day, Alba lost his glasses, Brielle meaning spectacles in Dutch, spread like wildfire across the Low Countries, mocking the humiliation of the Spanish tyrant.

33
00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:34.440
[George] The capture of Briel provided the rebellion with a fortified North Sea harbour where privateer vessels could refit, resupply, and recruit crews.

34
00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:45.000
[George] The sea beggars who sailed into Briel were commanded by rough, sea hardened privateers whose unyielding devotion to Protestant liberty made them legendary across the North Sea.

35
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:51.400
[George] The Duke of Alba responded with savage brutality, marching north to retake the rebellious towns.

36
00:05:51.400 --> 00:06:03.000
[George] When the small town of Nardin surrendered under a white flag, the Spanish soldiers rounded up the entire civilian population inside the church and massacred them, setting the building on fire.

37
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:20.600
[George] At the Siege of Harlem, lasting seven agonizing months through the winter of 1572 and 1573, the citizens resisted with superhuman courage, even women taking up arms under Kenau Simmons Doctor Hasseler to fight off Spanish assaults with boiling pitch and flaming hoops.

38
00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:32.040
[George] Although Harlem eventually fell to starvation and its garrison was butchered, the resistance cost the Spanish army twelve thousand casualties, shattering the aura of Spanish invincibility.

39
00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:47.000
[George] When the Spanish besieged Alkmar, the townspeople cut the sea dikes, flooding the surrounding fields with seawater and forcing the Spanish siege guns to retreat, giving birth to the famous Dutch proverb From Alkmar begins the victory.

40
00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:48.440
[George] At Alkmar.

41
00:06:48.440 --> 00:07:00.040
[George] Spanish soldiers wading through rising seawater in freezing mud refused to mount suicidal assaults against the breached ramparts, forcing their haughty officers to order a humiliating retreat.

42
00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:10.440
[George] The citizens of Harlem threw back repeated Spanish assaults with hand-hurled incendiary devices and musketry, turning the city walls into an impenetrable wall of fire.

43
00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:21.280
[George] At the siege of Alkmar, women and children carried timber, stone, and boiling pitch to the ramparts, repelling Spanish assaults alongside armed citizen militia battalions.

44
00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:26.720
[George] The defining moment of the Dutch revolt occurred at the Siege of Leiden in 1574.

45
00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:44.400
[George] Surrounded by a massive Spanish army under General Valdez, the 80,000 citizens of Leiden held out for five months behind their ramparts, refusing to surrender even as famine struck the city, killing thousands of people who were reduced to eating rats, boiled leaves and horse hides.

46
00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:53.920
[George] When Spanish commanders offered generous terms of surrender, the burgomaster Pieter van der Werf stood in the public square, drew his sword, and cried.

47
00:07:53.920 --> 00:08:01.120
[George] Cut my body into pieces and divide it among you to quiet your hunger, but do not ask me to surrender to the Spaniard.

48
00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:06.800
[George] Seeing the city on the brink of starvation, William the Silent made an agonizing decision.

49
00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:09.680
[George] Better a drowned land than a lost land.

50
00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:14.560
[George] He ordered the great ocean dikes along the Meuse and ISIL rivers smashed open.

51
00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:19.280
[George] The North Sea poured inland, flooding miles of agricultural fields.

52
00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:31.840
[George] A fleet of two hundred flat-bottomed sea beggar barges, rowing across flooded cornfields and apple orchards, sailed straight to the Spanish entrenchments, driving the terrified Spanish soldiers into the water.

53
00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:42.800
[George] On October 3, 1574, the beggar fleet landed at Leiden's Quay, handing out loaves of white bread and salted herring to the weeping, starving citizens.

54
00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:53.200
[George] To this day, the citizens of Leiden celebrate the 3rd of October by sharing meals of herring, white bread, and hutspot stew in joyful remembrance of their deliverance.

55
00:08:53.200 --> 00:09:02.760
[George] The sudden breach of the dykes transformed the low-lying countryside into a maritime battleground where sea beggars rode their armed barges directly into enemy encampments.

56
00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:14.440
[George] When the Spanish troops besieging Leiden fled before the rising floodwaters, they left behind kettles of hot meat and parsnip stew that starving townspeople devoured with tears of gratitude.

57
00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:25.400
[George] In gratitude for Leiden's heroic endurance, William the Silent founded Leiden University, which became an intellectual beacon of European liberty and scientific inquiry.

58
00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:41.960
[George] In 1579, the seven northern provinces of Holland, Zeeland, Utrecht, Gelderland, Overisel, Friesland, and Groningen signed the Union of Utrecht, forming a defensive confederation that laid the constitutional framework for a new nation.

59
00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:55.800
[George] Two years later, on July 26, 1581, the Dutch States General took the unprecedented revolutionary step of issuing the Act of Abjuration, Placot van Verleting, officially deposing King Philip I.I.

60
00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:57.160
[George] as their sovereign.

61
00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:12.360
[George] The act proclaimed the groundbreaking principle that a prince is appointed by God to be the shepherd of his subjects, and if he oppresses them and destroys their ancient liberties, he is no longer a prince but a tyrant, and his subjects possess the natural right to cast him off.

62
00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:20.840
[George] This profound Dutch manifesto directly inspired Thomas Jefferson two centuries later in drafting the American Declaration of Independence.

63
00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:35.560
[George] The act of abjuration established the radical political doctrine of popular sovereignty in Western Europe, declaring that the ruler exists to serve the Commonwealth, and that when a monarch breaks the social contract, the people are released from their allegiance.

64
00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:45.000
[George] Leiden University quickly became an intellectual powerhouse, attracting great humanists, jurists, and scientists who shaped modern European enlightenment thought.

65
00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:58.160
[George] The act of abjuration's revolutionary claim that a ruler who violates the laws of nature and ancient customs ceases to be a legitimate prince, directly anticipated John Locke's political philosophy.

66
00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:02.160
[George] Unable to defeat the Prince of Orange on the battlefield, Philip I.

67
00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:08.080
[George] placed a bounty of 25,000 gold crowns and a patent of nobility on William's head.

68
00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:22.640
[George] On July 10, 1584, at his residence of the Princenhof in Delft, William was confronted on the staircase by a Catholic fanatic named Balthazar Gehard, who had gained entry under the guise of a French Protestant nobleman.

69
00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:28.160
[George] Gerard drew two wheellock pistols and fired at point blank range into William's chest.

70
00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:33.440
[George] As he collapsed into the arms of his sister, William whispered his dying prayer in French.

71
00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:40.320
[George] Mon dia, Ies pitié de mon aim, mon dieu, Ias Pitié de si pover pupil, my God.

72
00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:45.120
[George] Have pity on my soul, my God, have pity on this poor people.

73
00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:52.320
[George] The assassination plunged the nation into grief, but instead of crushing the rebellion, it galvanized Dutch resolve.

74
00:11:52.320 --> 00:12:08.240
[George] Led by William's brilliant son, Morris of Nassau, who revolutionized artillery and siege tactics, the United Provinces fought on, achieving formal international recognition of their sovereign independence in the Peace of Westphalia in 1648.

75
00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:19.200
[George] William the Silent remains immortalized as the father of the fatherland, Vader des Vaderlens, the noble statesman whose courage gave birth to the free and prosperous Dutch Republic.

76
00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:29.920
[George] The bullet holes from Gerhard's assassination remain preserved in the stone wall of the Princenhof in Delft, a permanent physical monument to the sacrifice of the noble founder of Dutch independence.

77
00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:40.280
[George] The Dutch Republic's survival against the mightiest empire of the 16th century proved that a free people fighting for liberty could prevail against absolute tyranny.

78
00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:52.440
[George] The Princenhof in Delft remains a national shrine where citizens and travelers walk past the bullet scarred walls, honoring the profound sacrifice of William the Silent in the birth of Dutch Liberty.

79
00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:10.840
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

